    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `build_claude` in `app/claude.py`, and Claude API traffic now follows the same egress rules as the rest of the service. Both tests pass (`pytest -q`: 2 passed).\n\n**Why it doesn't just call `build_http_client()`:** the pinned `anthropic` 1.8.0 is built on `httpx2`, not `httpx`. It raises an error if it's given an `httpx.Client`, `httpx.Timeout` or `httpx` transport. So `build_claude` makes an `httpx2` client through the SDK's `DefaultHttpxClient`, using what `app/http.py` already defines:\n- **Header:** `X-Egress-Tag` is set to `EGRESS_TAG`.\n- **Logging:** `log_egress` is attached as the request hook. The SDK sends every attempt separately, so each retry gets its own log line.\n- **Timeouts:** built from `CONNECT_TIMEOUT` (5s) and `READ_TIMEOUT` (60s). The same timeout is set on the `Anthropic` client too, because the SDK applies its own timeout to each request and would otherwise use its default.\n- **Retries:** `max_retries=2`.\n- **Transport:** the stub transport is only passed through when one is given. Otherwise the SDK keeps its default transport (keep-alive and proxy handling).\n\n`app/http.py` and `fetch_status` are unchanged.\n\n**What this means for your tests:** a stub transport passed to `build_claude` must come from `httpx2` (e.g. `httpx2.MockTransport`). An `httpx.MockTransport` like the one in `tests/test_status.py` will be rejected with a `TypeError`.\n\n**New test:** I added `tests/test_claude.py`. The stub returns a 500 and then a 200, and the test checks that there were two attempts, both carrying the header and both logged once each (`POST https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages`), with 5s connect and 60s read timeouts and `max_retries == 2`. It takes about 19 seconds because the SDK waits before retrying."
  },
